[Music]  hey everyone thanks for tuning in and  welcome to my talk  my name is tom barkley and i am the lab  director at  trichome analytical  trichome is a third party cannabis  testing lab located in new jersey  and the topic of my talk today has to do  with  standardization  in cannabis  pesticide testing  particularly the standardization of  pesticide testing analytical methods  so today i'll be presenting a method  that i've been developing to meet the  aoac standard method performance  requirements  so pesticides of course refer to  various different types of chemicals  that are  intended to  prevent pests  the epa  definition is a little bit more broad  and does include other things like  plant regulators  defoliants desiccants and nitrogen  stabilizers  currently the epa has over  300 pesticide chemicals that are  registered for use  as pesticides  and currently there are 59  pesticide products that are registered  for use  by the epa specifically for hemp  and hemp of course is the only currently  federally legal  form of cannabis  and out of this 59 pesticide products  58  have active ingredients that are  considered to be bio pesticides  and the one pesticide chemical listed as  a conventional pesticide  has an  active ingredient of  potassium salts of fatty acids so these  are all relatively non-toxic  and relatively safe chemicals  and because of the legal status of  cannabis  in the united states it is very much  regulated on a state-by-state basis  with many of the states having their own  programs and actual pesticide rules  so many many times the states will  divide up their cannabis programs  based on either recreational  medical and hemp  and hemp is also sometimes referred to  as cbd or low thc  and i just wanted to highlight  the  medical cannabis market in particular  and uh do a quick comparison of the  different state pesticide testing rules  and  kind of highlight the issue that there  is with the lack of harmonization  between these states and their pesticide  rules  so this chart is from a paper published  last year in the current research and  toxicology  and in this study they did a survey of  the pesticide testing  regulations in jurisdictions  and states within the united states  and this is specifically for medical  cannabis programs  and what they found is that there is a  total of 98 insecticides that are  regulated across the 21  of the uh  the surveyed jurisdictions in the study  but almost half of these were only  regulated in a single jurisdiction out  of out of the total number  so basically  what this means is that every state kind  of has their own  pesticide  list for cannabis and so there is  definitely this lack of harmonization  between states  and then also in the same study  they did look at the differences in  action levels for the pesticides that  were regulated  by more than one state  and again  they found a pretty broad range of of  action limits um some of these uh  chemical pesticides you can see here  range in having action levels from  you know up to three orders of magnitude  difference between the different  programs and that's represented here  with these gray lines  with the median  action action level represented by the  the yellow  circle in these charts  and the lack of standardization also  exists for  specifically for  pesticide cannabis testing methods  analytical methods  and  shown here are the results from  an interlaboratory  pesticide and cannabis  proficiency test so this is the 2021  emerald test which is  specifically for pesticide and hemp  flour  so for this test each lab was supplied  with a spiking solution  containing the the pesticides to be  tested  as well as a blank hemp matrix material  and the labs were instructed to spike  the pesticides directly into the hemp  and then quantitate with  their own  methods that they utilize in  in each lab  and shown in this chart are the interlab  percent relative standard deviations  of the measured  concentrations for each pesticide in  this study  and these are listed  uh based on their assigned concentration  which is shown at the top of the graph  and you can see that there is a fair  amount of variability here so  approximately half of these  pesticide analytes have  a percent rst that is greater than 30.  and i think this probably can be  attributed to the fact that  each lab is pretty much running their  own  internally developed method and there is  no  standard method being utilized for this  type of analysis  and then also as the lab director at  trichome analytical  i have to deal with  pesticide testing pretty much every day  at the lab  we test various different types of  cannabis  hemp which comes from  different states throughout the country  we also test medical cannabis which  comes from  uh in-state in new jersey the new jersey  medical cannabis program  and we are also awaiting for  the  final testing regulations for new  jersey's recreational cannabis program  so there's definitely a lot to keep  track of as far as the regulations  and you know there is a lot of  complexity when it comes to  multi-residue pesticide analysis so  we are definitely always looking for  ways to try to  uh simplify our methods  and kind of harmonize the the different  testing methods that we utilize in our  lab so  recently we were looking for ways to get  involved in contributing towards the  development of a standardized method for  pesticides and cannabis  and there are a number of standards  organizations that are  getting more involved in cannabis and  one of those is aoac  aoac has a very active cannabis  program called the cannabis analytical  science program  or casp  and in 2018 they published a  standard method performance requirement  for  the quantitation of pesticides in  dried cannabis materials  so the purpose of an smpr  in general is to serve as a guide for  labs to submit possible methods for  consideration  as a standard method  and some of the highlights for the  for the smpr for pesticides in cannabis  in particular  is shown here and  there are a number of requirements such  as the  total number of pesticides to be tested  for in this  case there are 104  and within that list of 104 is pretty  much any  united states  requirement for an individual state  cannabis program and that includes  california  and also canada in that list  and there are a number of other  requirements such as the percent  recovery  repeatability  and reproducibility of the method  and then in addition  uh each pesticide has a recommended  action limit and a limit of quantitation  so we basically wanted to see if we  could  develop a method that would  meet all of these requirements in the  aoac smpr  and so as a starting point for this  project i wanted to  utilize our current  in-house lab method for pesticides in  cannabis  in that method you know we utilize both  gcms and lcms  those are both triple quad instruments  and we also use a single extraction step  and a relatively simple clean up process  for this  project we also were able to collaborate  with shimazu scientific and millipore  sigma for their help in the method  development process  so one of the first things that i wanted  to explore with this method was the  extraction solvent  and i had some prior experience  working with one of the pesticides in  the aoac list which is ethaphon  and ethaphon is not extracted very well  using acetonitrile so  i wanted to try to use methanol  methanol is commonly used in the lab  particularly for cannabis and extracting  cannabinoids  and what i found was that methanol does  actually work a lot better for  extracting ethaphon  from hemp compared to  acetonitrile and  the percent recovery that i measured for  acetonitrile was around 10 percent  but with methanol it was around  90 percent uh for for ethaphon being  extracted from hemp  and that's shown here in the bottom left  of the slide these are  lcms chromatograms  and you can also see here on the right  side of this slide  these are superimposed chromatograms of  epiphone  extracted with various mixes of  acetonitrile and methanol as the  extraction solvent  and you can see that as the  percentage of methanol increases  from  100  or from 10 methanol in the black trace  up to 100 methanol in the orange trace  uh that the peak response increases as  well which indicates the  better extraction efficiency  unfortunately the problem with methanol  is that it does appear to  co-extract some of those more  undesirable matrix components  that can be problematic such as  chlorophyll  and you can see here on the left  image this is a comparison of  the just the visual color of the extract  acetone nitrile versus methanol you  could see that the methanol extract has  a much darker green color  and then even after the cleanup  which  we're using a  c18 solid phase extraction cartridge  even after that process there's a lot of  that green  uh color left in the extract  for the for the methanol extract  and so for these reasons and the  difficulties with cleanup  for this method i decided to stay with  acetonitrile as the extraction solvent  and not use methanol  so  here is the sample preparation  uh steps for this method  so the method uses a  half a gram of cannabis laid out into a  centrifuge tube  three milliliters of acetonitrile is  added  which is acidified with one percent  acetic acid  after vortex mixing and centrifugation  the extract is cleaned up using a  c18 solid phase extraction  pass-through cartridge  the cartridge is then flushed  additionally with one milliliter of  acetonitrile  and that final diluted and cleaned  extract can be analyzed directly by  lcms and gcms  and then shown on the bottom left of  this slide are the different steps at  which the internal standard  and the pesticide spiking solutions are  added  depending on the sample type  so for the spikes  the spiking solution  containing the target pesticides as well  as the internal standard are added  directly to the cannabis before  extraction  whereas for the  calibration standards  the  the  spiking solution  and the internal standards are added  during step two  after the extraction solvent has been  added  the calibration for this method utilizes  an eight point calibration curve  the standards are matrix matched and  a total of 87 of the pesticides are  quantitated by lcms  with the remaining 15 being quantitated  by gcms  here are the instrument parameters for  lcms  the instrument that i used for this  method development is a shimadzu lcms  8060  with electrospray ionization  operating in both positive and negative  mode  the  the method here is a  reverse phase method with a two  microliter  injection volume  and the initial mobile phase condition  is 100 aqueous  and i found that that helped with the  with the elution of the polar pesticides  in this method  for gcms i used a  shimazu  tq8040nx  the injection volume was two microliters  and this is a splitless high pressure  injection  so in order to test the method  performance  i spiked the target pesticides in three  different  cannabis matrices  and i really wanted to see the  difference in performance for these  different types of cannabis  and those those different  types of cannabis are shown here so i  use a cbd biomass which has a relatively  low  concentration of  cannabinoids  due to the fact that's going to have  some more leaves and stems mixed in with  it  and then also i utilized a cbd flower  and a thc flower  the cannabinoid concentrations are shown  here these are tested  in-house with our  cannabinoid potency method  the spiking solution level  or  i should say the  pesticide spiking level in the cannabis  was 100 nanograms per gram  and for those pesticides having limits  of quantitation that were greater than  100 nanograms per gram i spiked at one  microgram per gram  and then also  the important thing to note here is that  the blank material that was used to  matrix match the calibration standards  was the cbd biomass  so here are the spike recovery results  for the first half  of the lcms pesticides  the second half is shown on the next  slide  and these results are ordered by the  retention time so the earlier alluding  pesticides are to the left the later  alluders are to the right  and the horizontal dotted lines  represent the aoac  acceptable range for the percent  recovery and that's between  60 and 120  and  overall you can see that the percent  recovery for the cbd biomass  is well within the acceptance limits  being between 60 and 120 percent  there were a few pesticides that are  outside of the acceptance range for the  cbd flower and the thc flower  and here are the results for the second  half of the lcms percent recovery data  so this is for the compounds that are  alluding later in the run between 5  minutes and 11 minutes  and there are are a few interesting  things going on  with this data  the first is that the cbd biomass again  is  uh the recoveries there are well within  the acceptance range however there are a  number of  results for the cbd and thc flowers that  are outside of the acceptance range  and  basically my interpretation of this is  that  this is due to the matrix suppression  and enhancement effects within the lcms  which is going to be dependent on the  actual strain  and i think this makes sense also given  the fact that  the  non-polar  co-extracted matrix components  such as the cannabinoids are  expected to be alluding in this part of  the run  as opposed to the earlier part of the  run which was shown in the previous  slide where the percent recoveries were  quite good  for all three of the matrices  and then again you can really see the  difference between the cbd biomass  which was  specifically used as the material to  matrix match the calibration standards  compared to the results for those flower  samples  and then moving on to the gcms  spike recovery results  you can see again that all of the tested  analytes  are within the acceptance range for the  cbd biomass  some of the later alluding pesticides  such as deltimethrin cyphermethrin and  ciphluthrin had low  unacceptable results for  the cbg flower and thc flower  so to summarize the performance of this  method  there were two of the aoac  pesticides of which could not be  quantitated and those were ethaphon and  a proteon  so epiphone i mentioned earlier that was  an issue with the extraction solvent  a proteon i found  to have a strong interference by both  gcms and lcms  in this chromatogram  on this slide which is which is gcms you  can see  a comparison between  the same level of a proteon in  acetonitrile versus  in a matrix extract  and there is a significant interference  there which may be due to the  cannabinoids  so out of the  pesticides they could be quantitated  with this method there are a total  of 102  and all of them that met the percent  recovery and repeatability requirements  of the aoac  smpr  for the single spike level that was  tested in this study  and the big caveat here is that the same  batch of hemp  or cannabis used for spiking also needed  to be used for the matrix matched  calibration standards  and then also for the  aoac recommended action limits or limits  of quantitation  there were a total of eight pesticides  which could not meet  the action limits that were specified by  aoic as specifically being  action limits that were based on a  survey of regulations  in the united states and canada which  was part of that smpr  the interesting thing about  these these eight pesticides here is  that they do  the the performance of this method  for for the limits of quantitation  for those pesticides  does meet the action levels for  uh various states that i've shown here  such as california  the new york cannabinoid hemp program  and also new jersey's interim testing  standards so you can see that the aoac  recommended limits are quite a bit lower  from what i'm from what i'm used to and  what is  you know commonly done for these states  so in conclusion uh overall i think that  this method is definitely useful for  labs  wanting to quantitate a broad range of  pesticides  meeting various state cannabis  requirements  however there were a few shortfalls in  meeting all of the requirements of the  alac  snpr  and  the biggest issue  in the method development was the low  limits of quantitation for some of the  pesticides which were hard to meet  and  basically my focus in the method  development  keeping these these limits of  quantitation in mind  was to try to increase the sensitivity  of the method as much as possible  and the way that i approached that here  was to not dilute the sample  too much  which would effectively increase the  amount of analyte  that is being seen by the detector  and i think that that has definitely  contributed towards  some of the issues i saw with the matrix  effects  differences between the different  strains  one other thing that  i should mention as well is that i did  see  uh i did see some issues with with the  uh gcms inlet getting dirty  after repeated injections  and so i think that a further dilution  could also benefit there um  as far as the the gcms repeatability  and overall again i think it's uh  important for the industry moving  forward to consider the fact that  cannabis is a complex matrix  and as i've seen in this method  development what works for one type of  strain  might not work for another especially  when it comes to pesticide testing  and any potential future developments of  a standardized method for pesticides in  cannabis should keep this in  consideration  so thank you very much for your  attention  i'd like to again thank shimazu  scientific and military sigma for this  help  for their help with this project  and uh please uh feel free to reach out  to me  at the email address listed here thank  you very much  you

